From 28ccaac65c4f875ffb72e1c3be5a5ba1cd70d5f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 04:05:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] GStreamer: multiplanar YUV no picture on unaligned plane
 stride

https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=328398

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

A hardware-decoded multiplanar YUV frame delivered as a DMABuf shows no
picture on panfrost/Mali even though decoding succeeds. importYUV()
splits the frame into one single-plane EGLImage per plane, each built
with that plane's own byte stride. A plane imported on its own is held to
the GPU's stricter standalone row alignment, 64 bytes on Mali arch >= 7,
whereas a true multiplanar image needs only 16. When a plane's stride is
a multiple of 16 but not of 64, an rk3588 AV1 luma stride of 592 at width
586 for example, the standalone import is rejected, importYUV() returns
null and nothing is painted. Widths that are multiples of 64 play. The
fault is a property of the plane byte stride, so it reaches every
multiplanar YUV format importYUV() handles, 10-bit P010 included.

Keep the per-plane path and WebKit's own YUV conversion as the single
colour path for every frame. When a plane's zero-copy import is rejected,
repack that plane into an aligned-stride single-plane GBM buffer object of
the same per-plane format, read through a GstVideoFrame map so the dma-buf
cache is synchronised, then import the repacked plane through the existing
path and combine it with the zero-copy planes. The colour result is
byte-identical to the per-plane render used today; the per-frame
difference is a copy of the misaligned plane, and 64-byte-aligned strides
keep the zero-copy import. The first rejected {format, modifier, plane,
stride} is cached so later frames of the stream repack directly and the
GPU logs its alignment error once.

